39,2,1,talk,Servas,,"My findings suggest that our reporting process is burdened by external dependencies. Hardware-based assets require costly, time-consuming appraisals, yet they offer the comfort of independently verifiable data, unlike software holdings which rely solely on self-reported figures.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,1,talk,Asuka,,"My findings confirm a disparity in stability. Our successful exits were quiet, back-office tools, while the write-off had high overhead. Furthermore, retention is strong for specialized infrastructure but weak for general retail tools.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,1,talk,Minako,,"The data is concerning. Our reserves are being depleted by follow-on calls at twice the projected rate, and the credit line is far more restrictive than I hoped, offering no value against subscription contracts.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,1,talk,Mio,,"Wow, things are looking a bit tricky! I found out that while we're great at filling support roles, finding field crews takes forever. Plus, our software partners are totally swamped with board seats!","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"The data confirms my suspicions. Deployment speed is heavily dictated by the customer's nature and the supply chain. Security reviews for regulated sectors are a significant bottleneck, and hardware dependencies introduce unpredictable delays.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Servas,,"It seems we are caught between two burdens: the heavy cost of verifying physical assets and the blind trust required for software. True peace comes from clarity, and I worry about the opacity of self-reported figures.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Servas, opacity is a risk, but the real danger is misinterpreting the source of our wins. Our exits came from essential back-office tools, not flashy growth. We must prioritize stability and high retention over narrative.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,2,talk,Minako,,"@Asuka, stability is only possible if we have the liquidity to maintain it. With follow-on calls draining our reserves so quickly, we cannot afford a candidate that requires heavy, unplanned capital injections.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,2,talk,Mio,,"@Minako, that sounds scary! But we also have to think about our time! If we pick a company that needs a ton of field staff or more software board seats, we're going to be totally exhausted!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Mio, exhaustion is a symptom of poor planning. The real risk is the gap between the plan and the first customer. If we invest in a regulated sector or hardware, we must accept a timeline that is months longer than promised.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Servas,,"It is wise to consider these risks. However, I find the prospect of blind trust in software dashboards unsettling. I would rather endure a slow, costly audit than a sudden, invisible collapse. Clarity is the foundation of trust.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Servas, a slow audit is a known cost, but churn is a silent killer. If we ignore the fact that general tools are losing customers during price hikes, we are simply choosing a different kind of blindness.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,3,talk,Minako,,"@Asuka, churn is indeed a killer, but so is a liquidity crisis. Since our credit line ignores subscription contracts, a software-heavy portfolio doesn't actually provide us with a safety net for borrowing. We are more exposed than we realize.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,3,talk,Mio,,"@Minako, that's a huge point! If we can't borrow against software, and our software partners are already too busy to help, we're in trouble! We need someone who doesn't need a million new hires right away!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"@Minako, your point on liquidity is noted. However, we must also consider that a ""safe"" software bet can still be stalled for nine months by a single security review. We cannot ignore the external friction that delays revenue.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Servas,,"We must seek a balance. While the costs of physical audits are high, the ability to verify figures independently brings a serenity that software cannot offer. Let us weigh these burdens carefully as we see the candidates.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,2,4,talk,Asuka,,"The data is clear: essential, specialized tools provide the only real stability. We must avoid any candidate that relies on a ""growth"" narrative or general retail tools, as those are the most vulnerable to churn.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,2,4,talk,Minako,,"I agree that we must avoid high-churn models. However, we must also avoid any candidate with heavy hardware or equipment needs, as those follow-on calls are draining our reserves far faster than we planned.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
57,2,4,talk,Mio,,I'm with you! Let's find a company that's lean and doesn't need a huge army of field workers or more of our partners' time. We need a win that doesn't leave us totally burnt out!,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,2,4,talk,Toshio,,"I will be looking for a candidate that avoids both the hardware supply chain and the regulatory bottlenecks of the financial or medical sectors. A lean, specialized software tool seems the most viable path.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
